That makes sense — it got auto-healed away. Your MIG's `defaultActionOnFailure: REPAIR` was set, so since the health check kept failing (`TIMEOUT`), GCP automatically killed that instance and tried to recreate a fresh one. That's why your SSH session died mid-diagnosis — the instance disappeared out from under you.

**This is actually a real problem** — we can't diagnose a boot-time bug if the platform keeps killing the evidence every few minutes. Let's stop that churn and debug on a stable, isolated test VM instead.

**Step 1 — stop the MIG from replacing instances while we investigate:**
```bash
gcloud compute instance-groups managed set-autoscaling cn-backend-mig-regional \
  --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 \
  --region=me-central2 \
  --min-num-replicas=1 \
  --max-num-replicas=1 \
  --target-cpu-utilization=0.6 \
  --cool-down-period=60
```

**Step 2 — create a standalone, non-MIG test VM from the same image** so nothing auto-heals it away while we look:
```bash
gcloud compute instances create cn-backend-debug-vm \
  --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 \
  --zone=me-central2-a \
  --machine-type=e2-medium \
  --image=cn-backend-image-fd726a3 \
  --network=default \
  --subnetwork=default \
  --no-address
```

**Step 3 — SSH into this stable debug VM:**
```bash
gcloud compute ssh cn-backend-debug-vm \
  --project=pg-ae-n-app-125609 \
  --zone=me-central2-a \
  --tunnel-through-iap
```

**Then run the same diagnostics — this instance won't disappear on us:**
```bash
ps aux | grep -i pm2
ps aux | grep -i node
sudo systemctl list-units --type=service | grep pm2
sudo journalctl -u 'pm2-*' --no-pager -n 100
```

Paste all output. This will finally show us why the app isn't starting — without the platform pulling the rug out mid-investigation.
